// Board pack: what the compiler knows about the board it emits for.
//
// The module name is generic on purpose. Every OS-free emit target carries its own copy at
// emitter/<TARGET>/board_pack.silica with the same exported functions and its own answers, the way
// each target carries its own shared/reg_conventions.silica; one compiler binary is built per TARGET,
// so the emitter that asks `board_pack@window_containing(...)` always asks about its own board. This
// copy describes the ESP32-S3_raw target on the port test board and is the draft for work item 14 of
// esp32s3_peek_poke_implementation_plan.md; it is not yet in the build.
//
// Runtime twin: board/runtime/rt_device.S (planned) walks the same window table for the run-time
// check of a non-constant map_device. When one changes, the other changes.
//
// Sources, all in this tree: board/PORT_TEST_BOARD_byu_idaho_v4.0_feb26.md (sheet, netlist and what
// was measured on the test unit), board/runtime/rt_board.S, rt_console.S, rt_start.S,
// rt_supervisors.S and silica_esp32s3.ld (every address), the bring-up apps board/apps/asm_02_rgb,
// asm_03_buttons, asm_04_buzzer, asm_08_gpio_diag, asm_10_pin_hunt, asm_11_addressable_leds (what was
// found on the hardware), and asm_13_display_hello with the read-back probes written for it on
// 2026-09-29 (the display section: controller ID, status, frame memory, and the two protocol rules). One row does not come from this tree: the SPI2 window (index 9) is from
// ESP-IDF's soc headers and is TRM-unconfirmed, marked by `window_base_verified`. The other two places where
// the TRM has the final word are marked below.
//
// Shape: pure functions, no allocation, no effects. Tables are indexed (`window_base(i)`), so the
// emitter reads a row without building a list; a device description is still the programmer's
// `impl fn registers(device: (:tag))` (device spec §4.9) -- the register rows here record what the
// runtime drives today and are the material for writing the first ones.
//
// Known runtime defect met while writing asm_13 (2026-09-29), not a board fact but worth knowing
// when a driver is written for this target: a chain of eight nested CALL8 frames faults, and the
// fault hook then faults itself (`[silica] fault at` inside silica_rt_actor_fault_hook's s32c1i), so
// the reported address is the hook's, not the original. asm_05's 1000-deep recursion passes, so it
// is not plain window overflow; unexplained. asm_13 keeps its drawing path seven frames deep.

